{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You shall not cook a kid in its mother's milk.</b> Regarding the reason for this commandment, most of the commentators set out to gather explanations and found in it no sufficient reason, to the point that some say this commandment is among the ḥukkim that have no reason known to us, and some say that originally it was a practice of idolatry, that they would cook meat in milk at their festivals, and therefore this commandment was juxtaposed to the festivals of the year. And some bring proof from what is written (Genesis 4:4), ״וְהֶבֶל הֵבִיא גַם הוּא מִבְּכֹרוֹת צֹאנוֹ וּמֵחֶלְבֵיהֶן״ — that they were accustomed to perform idolatrous worship with meat in milk. And it is plausible to hear that this commandment concerns the matter of the prohibition of mixed kinds (כִּלְאַיִם) and the intermingling of forces, for it is known that the flesh of the fetus is born from the blood of the female — the red [blood] that generates all redness, for from there is its source. And likewise the milk of the animal also has its source from the blood, for the blood curdles and becomes milk. And according to this, from the blood of the animal there came forth both the flesh of the fetus and the milk, for from there they separated, and it is not proper to go back and intermingle them by means of cooking, and likewise by means of eating, for eating is also cooking within the stomach (אִצְטוֹמְכָא). And if one soaked it all day in milk, there is no prohibition of ״לֹא תְבַשֵּׁל״, for they do not return to their original intermingling by means of soaking as they do by means of cooking in a pot and cooking within the stomach. And specifically when one eats meat first, for it does not cook in the stomach quickly enough before the milk arrives — but the milk cooks quickly before the meat arrives, and there is not such an intermingling; therefore there is no prohibition to eat meat after it. And the reason of this intermingling applies to all animal flesh, and ״גְּדִי בַּחֲלֵב אִמּוֹ״ is cited as the paradigm, for in it this intermingling is found intrinsically and in the first instance." "And in parashat Re'eh (Deuteronomy 14:21) [the Torah] juxtaposed this commandment to the prohibitions of forbidden foods, to tell you that not cooking alone did the Torah forbid, but eating as well. And here it juxtaposed it to the commandment of first fruits, for the fruits that ripened first are not fit to intermingle with the fruits that ripened afterward — rather, those that ripened first you shall bring to the house of YHWH your Elohim, and the remainder shall be yours. So too, one may not intermingle the blood that was 'cooked' first, from which the fetus was formed, with the blood that was 'cooked' afterward, from which the milk was formed — for once YHWH desired their separation, He does not desire their intermingling."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase אִי תָּרוּ לֵיהּ כּוּלָּא יוֹמָא בַּחֲלָבָא לֵית בֵּהּ לָאו דְּ״לֹא תְבַשֵּׁל״ is rendered literally: 'if one soaked it all day in milk, there is in it no prohibition of \"you shall not cook.\"' This paraphrases the Talmudic principle (Hullin 97b–98a) that soaking does not constitute the biblical prohibition of cooking."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:39.216505698Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}